  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1238 AM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nFriday and Saturday...Upper low center will remain over the Pacific \nNW both days, while the broad upper high to the east starts to \namplify and ease back towards CO. Models indicate that the moisture \nplume across Mexico thanks to Hurricane Marie will advect into CO, \nleading to scattered convection over the higher terrain, and more \nisolated activity for portions of the plains. It will continue to be \nhot both days, with highs in the 80s for the high valleys, which is \nabout 5 degrees above normal, and upper 80s to upper 90s for the \nplains, which is close to 10 degrees above normal.\n\nSunday and Monday...The Pacific NW low pressure system begins to \nopen up and rotate a shortwave across the Great Basin and Rocky Mt \nregion into Monday. This upper level dynamics will tap into the \navailable moisture plume to kick off greater coverage of afternoon \nand evening showers and thunderstorms for all of the higher terrain \nand I-25 Corridor, though the greatest probability will lie along \nthe Continental Divide. Flash flood potential for area burn scars \nwill start to increase starting Sunday. As for temps, it will still \nbe hot, but highs are forecast to be a few degrees cooler due to \nincreased cloud cover and active precip.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...The upper ridge to the east essentially \nremains in position so no real change in the pattern there. The low \npressure system over the Pacific NW continues to open and dumbbell \nacross the Intermountain West, then the trough starts to dig south \nacross the Great Basin. If this solution holds true, moisture \nadvection into CO increases leading to greater precip coverage and \ncooling temps. Stay tuned. Moore",
